@@s001 Polymorphism and map
@@s002 dato:list of A,ora:f at work,ok:list of B
@@s003 <code>map[A, B](l: List[A])(f: A =&gt; B): List[B]</code>. In the definition f is a hole: what it does is decided by whoever calls. Here <code>f = abs</code>, so A = Int and B = Int.
@@s004 First cell: abs(1) = 1.
@@s005 abs(−2) = 2.
@@s006 abs(3) = 3. Same length, same order: map changes the values, never the shape.
@@s007 Same map, another f: <code>_.toString</code>. Now B = String: from List[Int] comes out List[String]. The code of map has not changed.
@@s008 no:fixed type,dato:placeholder A,due:placeholder B,ok:use with different types
@@s009 ora:f (the hole),dato:leaves before,ok:leaves after
@@s010 dato:starting list,ok:result
@@s011 <code>
@@s012 Polymorphism and map
@@s013 <code>map</code> applies a function to each element of a list and returns the list of results. Writing it <b>just once for all types</b> is called parametric polymorphism: <code>[A, B]</code> are types chosen by whoever calls.
@@s014 From a <code>map</code> for Ints to one for all
@@s015 Removing Int from the signature (slide 8)
@@s016 A <b>monomorphic</b> <code>map</code>: it works only with lists of integers.
@@s017 But the body does <b>nothing</b> specific with the integers: it does not add, it does not compare. It only calls <code>f</code>.
@@s018 So I replace the types with placeholders: <code>A</code> for the starting elements, <code>B</code> for the arriving ones. The body stays identical.
@@s019 Same function, different types. A and B can also be equal.
@@s020 Do not confuse
@@s021 This is <b>parametric</b> polymorphism (generics). The polymorphism of classes, where an overridden method is chosen at runtime, is another thing: it is called <b>dynamic dispatch</b> (<a href="s03-classi-dispatch.html">week 3</a>).
@@s022 From Int to placeholders A and B
@@s023 <code>map</code> step by step
@@s024 map(List(1, -2, 3))(abs)
@@s025 <code>f</code> = <code>abs</code>. The list has one cell.
@@s026 <code>Cons</code> case: new cell with <code>f(h)</code>, and as the rest the <code>map</code> of the rest.
@@s027 Same thing on the second cell.
@@s028 Third cell.
@@s029 <code>map</code> of the empty list is the empty list.
@@s030 Same length, same order, values transformed. The original list has not changed.
@@s031 The hole f, filled twice
@@s032 "f: A => B means everything and nothing": where is it decided what f does?
@@s033 Right: inside the definition of <code>map</code>, <code>f</code> is a <b>hole</b>. It is filled <b>at the moment of the call</b>. Follow the colour of <code>f</code>.
@@s034 The hole f is filled at the call
@@s035 In the definition (exercise 4, week 3) <code>f</code> is just a name. We do not know what it will do.
@@s036 <b>Here</b> you decide: <code>f</code> = <code>_.toString</code>, that is <code>x => x.toString</code>. So A = Int and B = String.
@@s037 It is a <code>Branch</code>: the same <code>f</code> is passed to the two branches.
@@s038 In the leaves <code>f(value)</code> becomes <code>value.toString</code>.
@@s039 With another call, for example <code>map(albero)(_ * 10)</code>, the same definition would do something quite different.
@@s040 f is filled at the moment of the call
@@s041 The ready-made functions of the library
@@s042 From week 3 the lists of the standard library are used, where these functions are <b>methods</b>: they are called with the dot.
@@s043 Method
@@s044 Does
@@s045 Example
@@s046 Result
@@s047 <code>map(f)</code>
@@s048 transforms each element
@@s049 <code>List(1,2,3).map(_ * 2)</code>
@@s050 <code>List(2,4,6)</code>
@@s051 <code>filter(p)</code>
@@s052 keeps the elements for which p is true
@@s053 <code>List(1,2,3).filter(_ &gt; 1)</code>
@@s054 <code>List(2,3)</code>
@@s055 <code>flatMap(f)</code>
@@s056 f gives a list per element, then joins them
@@s057 <code>List(1,2).flatMap(x => List(x, x))</code>
@@s058 <code>List(1,1,2,2)</code>
@@s059 <code>take(n)</code>
@@s060 the first n
@@s061 <code>List(1,2,3).take(2)</code>
@@s062 <code>List(1,2)</code>
@@s063 <code>takeWhile(p)</code>
@@s064 the prefix while p holds
@@s065 <code>List(1,2,5,1).takeWhile(_ &lt; 3)</code>
@@s066 <code>List(1,2)</code>
@@s067 <code>forall(p)</code>
@@s068 does p hold for all?
@@s069 <code>List(2,4).forall(_ % 2 == 0)</code>
@@s070 <code>true</code>
@@s071 <code>exists(p)</code>
@@s072 does p hold for at least one?
@@s073 <code>List(1,3).exists(_ % 2 == 0)</code>
@@s074 <code>false</code>
